Yes, separating the logic into a shared core helper and thin provider-specific wrapper scripts keeps the original 3-argument interface completely intact while eliminating duplicate code.

Architecture Overview

• Core Engine (GitCloneCore.sh): Holds all the .netrc creation, trap cleanup, chmod, and git clone logic. It accepts the provider domain as its first argument.
• Provider Wrappers (GitClone.sh): Lightweight, provider-specific entry points (e.g., stored in provider subdirectories or named per provider) that hardcode the domain and forward $1, $2, and $3 directly to the core script.

1. Shared Core Script (GitCloneCore.sh)

Save this in a shared helpers location (such as ${BUILD_HOME}/helpers/git/GitCloneCore.sh).
sh
#!/bin/sh
##################################################################################
# SCRIPT      : GitCloneCore.sh
# AUTHOR      : Peter Winter (Refactored Core Engine)
# DESCRIPTION : Shared execution engine for shallow Git clones across providers.
# INTERFACE   : $0 <provider_domain> <owner_name> <repo_name> [auth_username]
##################################################################################
set -e

status () {
	${BUILD_HOME}/helpers/services/LogStatusMessages.sh "${1}"
}

BUILD_HOME="`/bin/cat /home/buildhome.dat`"

if [ "${1}" = "" ] || [ "${2}" = "" ] || [ "${3}" = "" ]
then
        status "Error: Core helper requires <provider_domain> <owner_name> <repo_name> [auth_username]"
        exit 1
fi

provider_domain="${1}"
repository_ownername="${2}" 
repository_name="${3}" 
repository_username="${4}"

# Set provider-specific default login if optional username was omitted
if [ "${repository_username}" = "" ]
then
        case "${provider_domain}" in
                *gitlab*)    repository_username="oauth2" ;;
                *bitbucket*) repository_username="x-token-auth" ;;
                *)           repository_username="git" ;;
        esac
fi

cleanup() {
        /bin/rm -f "${HOME}/.netrc"
}
trap cleanup EXIT

cat << EOF > "${HOME}/.netrc"
machine ${provider_domain}
login ${repository_username}
password ${REPOSITORY_TOKEN}
EOF

chmod 600 "${HOME}/.netrc"

if ! /usr/bin/git clone --depth 1 "https://${provider_domain}/${repository_ownername}/${repository_name}.git"
then
        error_code="$?"
        status "git clone exited with error code ${error_code}"
        exit 1
fi
Use code with caution.

2. Provider Wrapper Scripts (Original Interface Preserved)

Each provider script keeps the exact <owner_name> <repo_name> [auth_username] interface so existing callers do not break.

GitHub Wrapper (github/GitClone.sh)

sh
#!/bin/sh
# INTERFACE : $0 <owner_name> <repo_name> [auth_username]
BUILD_HOME="`/bin/cat /home/buildhome.dat`"
exec "${BUILD_HOME}/helpers/git/GitCloneCore.sh" "github.com" "$@"
Use code with caution.

GitLab Wrapper (gitlab/GitClone.sh)

sh
#!/bin/sh
# INTERFACE : $0 <owner_name> <repo_name> [auth_username]
BUILD_HOME="`/bin/cat /home/buildhome.dat`"
exec "${BUILD_HOME}/helpers/git/GitCloneCore.sh" "gitlab.com" "$@"
Use code with caution.

Bitbucket Wrapper (bitbucket/GitClone.sh)

sh
#!/bin/sh
# INTERFACE : $0 <owner_name> <repo_name> [auth_username]
BUILD_HOME="`/bin/cat /home/buildhome.dat`"
exec "${BUILD_HOME}/helpers/git/GitCloneCore.sh" "bitbucket.org" "$@"
Use code with caution.

Why exec ... "$@" Works Best Here

• Preserves Arguments Safely: "$@" passes $1, $2, and optional $3 cleanly without word-splitting issues, even if an argument is empty or contains spaces.
• Clean Process Replacement: exec replaces the wrapper shell process with GitCloneCore.sh, preserving exit codes and avoiding unnecessary subshell overhead.
If you would like to apply this same core + wrapper pattern to your other Git scripts (such as GitPush.sh, GitPull.sh, or GitCheckout.sh), share one of them and I can help structure the shared helper.
